  "posture_enforced": false,
  "text": "P1 \u2014 **RESHAPE**: container-as-the-install collides with the host reach the plan says agents need, and dogma-2 stays native until Phase 3; a uv pin plus prebuilt adapter bundles removes the measured drift without a VM.\n\nP2 \u2014 **RESHAPE**: a container `$HOME` of `/Users/<name>` is false symmetry on Linux, and default read-write `~/notes` on Syncthing is a fleet-wide blast radius.\n\nP3 \u2014 **KILL**: \"run every hook once with a no-op payload\" goes green while the guard is not enforcing, and the hub can stay up with the guard gone.\n\nP4 \u2014 **RESHAPE**: an image copy of Seed plus a host-native scheduler is still two Seeds, and the host still needs Python, so \"only a container runtime\" is false later in the same plan.\n\nP5 \u2014 **KILL**: `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` in the container environment is visible to `docker inspect`, `/proc`, and every agent child.\n\nP6 \u2014 **RESHAPE**: CI image builds are the right place to bump adapters; \"newest model at build time\" changes lane behaviour on every green rebuild.\n\nP7 \u2014 **RESHAPE**: Phase 0's falsifiers certify a prompt round-trip, and Phase 3's Unix-socket bridge does not cross Docker Desktop's VM.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. **\"refuses to start the hub if any hook cannot execute.\"** Executability is not enforcement. A hook that exits 0 on an empty or unmatched payload (the usual path when the matcher is `Write`) satisfies the check while every real write is allowed. A missing `.claude/settings.json` is zero hooks, and zero failures is a pass. **Failure:** doctor is green, the memory-mesh guard is off, writes proceed. **Fix:** drive a real PreToolUse deny through Claude Code's own runner and assert the target file is unchanged. Treat an empty hook set, an always-allow hook, and a timeout as start failure. Re-read settings on change and exit the process. A compose healthcheck only marks the container unhealthy; `restart: unless-stopped` keeps serving. Whether a missing interpreter fails open or locks all tools is unverifiable from the text; an always-allow hook is a fail-open either way.\n\n2. **\"Hooks must never fail open \u2014 in EITHER scope.\"** Those hooks live in Claude settings. Codex, Grok, and Antigravity get the same write mounts and never run them. **Failure:** the guarded pane is the only constrained one; the other three are a side door. **Fix:** enforce writes at the mount (read-only default, or a small outbox), not inside one vendor's hook.\n\n3. **\"The container gets `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` injected at start.\"** That value lands in the container config and the hub environment. `docker inspect` shows it. Every child inherits it, so any pane's shell can read `/proc/1/environ`. All four CLIs share one UID, so a mode-600 file in `corral-home` is also readable by the other panes; the environment is worse because it leaks with no path and into crash logs, for the whole `unless-stopped` lifetime. Docker Desktop starts at login, which can be before the fscrypt vault unlocks; a failed read then either crash-loops or the shim bakes the token into `compose.yaml` in plaintext. This replaces a Keychain ACL. **Failure:** one tool call exfiltrates the Claude account credential. **Fix:** separate process tree per lane; a host helper injects the token only into the Claude exec, never into the hub environment or the compose file.\n\n4. **\"a container agent's write reaches every host.\"** `~/notes` is read-write by default, and Syncthing propagates edits and deletes. dogma-2 also extra-mounts `~/Github`. Phase 0 runs this beside the native hub, both with hostname `dogma-2`. **Failure:** one injected or buggy call wipes or poisons the second brain on every machine, rewrites source, and breaks the single-writer rule while looking like one host. **Fix:** default the notes mount to read-only. The writable surface is a non-synced outbox a person promotes.\n\n5. **\"the container's `$HOME` equals the host's.\"** On dogma-2 that is `/Users/<name>`. The Linux image has no such passwd entry; `getpwuid` fails; tools that ignore `$HOME` write somewhere else or crash. Docker Desktop's share is case-insensitive APFS and the container is case-sensitive, so `Notes.md` and `notes.md` collide on the host and one file is destroyed. macOS ACLs are invisible inside the VM. Compose has no glob, so \"`~/.venvs/*`\" does not shadow every venv. `~` in a compose file expands on the host, and a short path mounts the wrong side. GID 20 in Linux is not macOS `staff`. \"Docker Desktop maps this on macOS\" is unverifiable from the text. **Failure:** silent file loss, bad ownership, and a Mach-O interpreter left on the path the hook fix thought it covered. **Fix:** fixed container home (`/corral`) and path translation in the shim. List every shadow mount. Prove case-folding and `pwd` on Docker Desktop before any same-path claim.\n\n6. **\"The pairing gate and WebAuthn origin stay exactly as today.\"** The browser origin can remain `http://127.0.0.1:8098`, and the WebAuthn ceremony stays in the browser if that string is unchanged. The peer address cannot: published ports arrive from the bridge gateway, so a 127.0.0.1 allowlist rejects the user or, once loosened, trusts the bridge. `localhost` often resolves to `::1` while the publish is IPv4-only. `corral pair` via `docker exec` bypasses the gate; the Docker socket is the real root boundary. `host.docker.internal` (needed for Ollama) also reaches the Mac's other loopback services, so \"bound to 127.0.0.1\" on the host is open from any agent. **Fix:** record the peer address the hub sees; publish v4 and v6; egress allowlist to the Ollama port only.\n\n7. **\"secret broker over a mounted unix socket.\"** A macOS Unix-domain socket does not connect across Docker Desktop's Linux VM, and USB serial for the rig does not pass through. The text offers no other bridge. **Failure:** \"retires the launchd plist\" has no mechanism on dogma-2, so native mode is permanent and the container is a second product. **Fix:** a host TCP helper with its own auth, or keep the daily seat native. The socket fact is platform knowledge; the plan asserts the mount and stops.\n\n8. **\"A host needs only a container runtime.\"** The same plan leaves Seed's scheduler host-native because it reconciles launchd/systemd, and names that the one place the host still needs Python (`uv`, 3.12). `corral init` runs `install.py` inside the container, where `launchctl` is absent, so service registration no-ops or writes units the host never loads. `corral update` runs `--update` on the live bind mount and can put the absolute venv path back. Whether `install.py` registers services is unverifiable from the text; running it in the container is what the plan specifies. **Fix:** copy Seed files from the image; register schedulers on the host; make the workspace swap atomic and re-run the deny test before traffic.\n\n9. **\"`home` | all of `~`\".** That includes `~/.ssh`, cloud credentials, and on a Mac `~/Library` (tokens, mail, cookies) plus iCloud dataless files that hang on read. Shadow volumes hide a few binaries and leave the secrets. `doctor` \"lists\" dangling symlinks; the text never says start fails. **Failure:** the power scope is full secret exposure and a virtiofs meltdown, with doctor reporting the dangling link and continuing. **Fix:** an allowlist of directories. Dangling symlinks abort start.\n\n10. **\"Lane defaults follow the newest model.\"** A scheduled rebuild that survives one prompt retags the image and moves every consult that omits `--model` onto a new model and effort. Newest is sometimes a preview with a different tool schema. **Failure:** Monday's hub is not Tuesday's hub, cost and answers move, CI is green. **Fix:** pin model and effort in a change a human merges. CI opens the bump.\n\n## Container vs a pinned host install\n\nThe measured pains are an unpinned interpreter, two service templates, a gitignored `npm install`, per-OS binaries, an unpinned second Seed clone, a workspace path mismatch, and stale lane defaults. `uv` with Python 3.12, one installer that unpacks CI-built tarballs of the adapters and the four CLIs for the host OS/arch, one pinned Seed tag, and `CORRAL_WORKSPACE` removes those. Launchd and systemd stay, so Keychain, `rigctl`, notifications, and the secret broker keep working. No VM at login, no `/Users` home inside Linux, no second login, no token in an environment. CI can still run `selftest_*.mjs` before those tarballs are published.\n\nThat host install has no filesystem sandbox. The sandbox is the only thing the container adds, and it is the part this plan gets wrong: read-write synced notes, one UID for every lane, open egress via `host.docker.internal`. Grok's Linux arm64 build is already marked unverified, so the image is not yet known to exist for dogma-2. Ship the pinned host install as the default. Offer the container as an optional sandbox after mounts are read-only, credentials are per lane, and egress is closed.\n\n## Tests the plan can pass while broken\n\n1. **Guard still open.** Removing the interpreter refuses start (the plan's test). Then restore a hook that exits 0 for every payload, and a workspace with no settings file. Both must refuse. Issue a real Claude Code call the guard must block and assert the file is unchanged. After start, point the hook at `/bin/true` and assert the hub process exits. Unhealthy-but-running is a fail.\n2. **Other panes.** From Codex or Grok in the same container, write and delete under the notes mount. The default install must make that write fail.\n3. **Token leak.** With a vault token present, assert it is absent from `docker inspect`, PID 1's environ, a Grok child's environ, and on-disk `compose.yaml`.\n4. **Mac mount truth.** On Docker Desktop, create two filenames that differ only by case from inside the container and confirm both survive on the host, owned by the host user. Run `pwd.getpwuid` and `Path.home()`. Confirm a macOS Unix socket is not connectable from the container.\n5. **Loopback and egress.** Pair in a browser via `127.0.0.1` and via `::1`. Fail if the gate treats the bridge peer as loopback. From an agent shell, a decoy port on the host other than Ollama must be unreachable.\n\n## What would change my mind\n\nA deny test that runs through each agent, fails closed on a missing or always-allow hook, and is backed by a read-only mount the other CLIs cannot write, with the hub process exiting when that test fails after startup, would reverse the P3 kill.",
